today on campaign check the  conservatives promise to get brexit done  if Boris Johnson wins a majority on  Thursday  he says he'll ask Parliament to approve  the withdrawal agreement before  Christmas and on January the 31st the UK  will leave the EU so in the narrowest  sense brexit will be done but we will  not be done with brexit having agreed to  leave the UK will then be in a  transition period in which EU rules  apply still a pie until December 2020  that gives the Prime Minister just 11  months to negotiate and ratify a trade  deal with the EU in fact the timetable  is even shorter because if the UK wanted  to extend the transition a decision has  to be made at the start of July  now the PM insists he won't ask for an  extension but he said that about  extending EU membership beyond October  the 31st this year so will he do it  again recently you trade negotiations  suggest a deal will take much longer  deals with Ukraine and South Korea took  9 years a deal with Canada considered  the model for the UK took 7 and deals  with Japan and Singapore took 5 years  each all of which suggests 11 months is  heroic ly ambitious and arguably  unrealistic the UK and the EU both want  a free trade agreement but a deal will  be hugely complex with numerous issues  to resolve in exchange for zero tariffs  to help manufacturing the EU will want  trade offs for access to its markets the  UK wants to move away from EU regulation  but divergence as it's known is likely  to lead to more checks and more red tape  for example if the UK changed standards  for food to help a separate deal with  the u.s. the EU might want to prevent  access scores of other issues like  access to UK fishing waters the NHS  security service industries and energy  could all be in play and even if all of  that is resolved the deal will still  have to be ratified by EU Parliament's  and here at Westminster all of this  matters because the EU accounts for 49  percent of UK trade and if there's no  deal and no transition the current EU UK  relationship would fall away with  dramatic consequences for the economy  Boris Johnson's campaign has been built  on delivering  exit but victory this week would only be  the start to finish the job on his terms  he'll have to complete a trade deal in  record time it's not impossible if both  sides agree to compromise but most trade  experts believe the Prime Minister will  again have to contemplate no deal or  break another promise and seek an  extension
 

